%[text] %[text:anchor:T_CFF3DFB5] # Air Track Collisions Lab: Pre-Lab
%[text] [⇦ Return to the main menu](<matlab: open(fullfile(currentProject().RootFolder,'MainMenu.m'))>)
%%
%[text] #### **How to measure velocity from the time recorded by a photogate stop clock**
%[text] When a cart passes beneath a photogate sensor, the time elapsed while the full length of the cart passes through is recorded by the stop clock. This implies you can calculate the speed of the cart by dividing the length of the cart by the time elapsed:
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\text{speed} = \\frac{ \\text{cart length}}{\\text{time recorded by stop clock }}${"altText":"\text{speed} = \frac{ \text{cart length}}{\text{time recorded by stop clock }}"}
%[text] To compute the velocity, you must also assign a direction. Carts traveling away from the pulley (to the left) are defined to have a negative velocity. Carts traveling to the right (toward the pulley) have a positive velocity.
%[text] **Example**. A cart passes through gate 2, traveling toward the right. The stop clock records a time of 0.5159 seconds. What is the velocity in m/s?
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![Illustration of how the stop clock works. When the cart reaches the sensor, the stop clock starts. When the cart leaves the sensor, the time recording stops.](text:image:7748)
%[text]{"align":"center"} *Illustration of how the stop clock works. When the cart reaches the sensor, the stop clock starts. When the cart leaves the sensor, the time recording stops.*
%[text] **Solution.**
%[text] The cart is 15 cm long. The amount of time required for the full length of the cart to pass through is 0.5159 s. This implies that the speed is
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\text{speed} = \\frac{ 0.15 \\text{ m }}{ 0.5159 \\text{ s}} \\approx 0.29 \\text{ m/s}${"altText":"\text{speed} = \frac{ 0.15 \text{ m }}{ 0.5159 \text{ s}} \approx 0.29 \text{ m\/s}"}
%[text] Because the cart is traveling to the right (toward the pulley), the velocity is positive:
%[text]{"align":"center"} $v =  0.29 \\text{ m/s}${"altText":"v =  0.29 \text{ m\/s}"}
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_8D9863F6] #### **Question 1** 
%[text] Suppose that stop clock records a time of 0.6 seconds when a cart passes through gate 1 heading toward gate 2. What was the velocity of the cart in m/s?
v_q1 = 0.25; % Replace NaN with the speed in m/s
%[text] **Note**: replace the **NaN** with your answer.
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_97804193] #### **Question 2**
%[text] Suppose that the carts have no additional weights and have been equipped with rubber bands and flags (resulting in elastic collisions). If cart 1 begins at rest and cart 2 approaches with a velocity of -0.3 m/s, what velocities will the carts have after collision?
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![Diagram of a horizontal air track with two carts. The left cart is stationary at 0 m/s. The right cart is moving left at −0.3 m/s, indicated by an orange arrow pointing left. The track is aligned along the x-axis, shown by a right-pointing axis arrow beneath the track.](text:image:43e2)
%[text] Since the carts can only move in 1D, your answer only requires the $x${"altText":"x"}-component: $v\_x${"altText":"v\_x"}.
%[text] **Solution:**
%[text] Conservation of momentum:
%[text] $v\_{1f} + v\_{2f} = -0.3${"altText":"v\_{1f} + v\_{2f} = -0.3"}
%[text] Conservation of energy:
%[text] $0.09  = v\_{1f}^2 + v\_{2f}^2${"altText":"0.09  = v\_{1f}^2 + v\_{2f}^2"}
%[text] Solving the system:
%[text] $v\_{1f} = - 0.3 \\text{ m/s}, \\ \\ v\_{2f} = 0 \\text{ m/s}${"altText":"v\_{1f} = - 0.3 \text{ m\/s},   v\_{2f} = 0 \text{ m\/s}"}
v1_q2 = -0.3; % The final velocity of cart 1 
v2_q2 = 0; % The final velocity of cart 1 
%[text] **Hint:** Use conservation of momentum and conservation of kinetic energy.
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_23CB4FF4] #### **Question 3**
%[text] Repeat question 2 but suppose that two additional 100 g weights were added to cart 2.
%[text] **Solution:**
%[text] Conservation of momentum:
%[text] $180v\_{1f} + 380v\_{2f} = -114${"altText":"180v\_{1f} + 380v\_{2f} = -114"}
%[text] Conservation of energy:
%[text] $34.2  = 180v\_{1f}^2 + 380v\_{2f}^2${"altText":"34.2  = 180v\_{1f}^2 + 380v\_{2f}^2"}
%[text] Solving the system:
%[text] $v\_{1f} = -\\frac{57}{140} \\text{ m/s}, \\ \\ v\_{2f} = -\\frac{3}{28} \\text{ m/s}${"altText":"v\_{1f} = -\frac{57}{140} \text{ m\/s},   v\_{2f} = -\frac{3}{28} \text{ m\/s}"}
v1_q3 = -57/140 % The final velocity of cart 1 
v2_q3 = -3/2 % The final velocity of cart 2 
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_55ADFD7A] #### **Question 4**
%[text] Suppose that the rubber bands have been replaced with wax/needle pairs (resulting in the carts sticking together after collision). Cart 1 has a single extra 100 g mass, while cart 2 has no extra masses. Suppose the carts are accelerated toward each other with cart 1 having a velocity of 0.4 m/s and cart 2 having a velocity of -0.5 m/s, what will their velocities be after collision?
%[text] **Solution:**
%[text] $v\_{f} = \\frac{m\_1 v\_{1,i} +  m\_2 v\_{2,i} }{m\_1 + m\_2} = \\frac{ 280(0.4) + 180(-0.5) }{280 + 180} = \\frac{11}{230} \\text{ m/s}${"altText":"v\_{f} = \frac{m\_1 v\_{1,i} +  m\_2 v\_{2,i} }{m\_1 + m\_2} = \frac{ 280(0.4) + 180(-0.5) }{280 + 180} = \frac{11}{230} \text{ m\/s}"}
v1_q4 = 11/230 % The final velocity of cart 1 
v2_q4 = 11/230 % The final velocity of cart 2 
%[text] **Hint:** The two carts will have the same velocity after collision since they are fused together.
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_CC74B952] #### **Question 5**
%[text] Suppose a mass weighing 150 g is dropped from a height of 2 meters above the ground. What will its speed be at a height of 0.5 m above the ground?
%[text] **Solution:**
%[text] $mgh = \\frac{1}{2}mv^2${"altText":"mgh = \frac{1}{2}mv^2"}
%[text] $(9.81)(1.5) = \\frac{1}{2}v^2${"altText":"(9.81)(1.5) = \frac{1}{2}v^2"}
%[text] $v = 5.42 \\text{ m/s}${"altText":"v = 5.42 \text{ m\/s}"}
v_q5 = sqrt(29.43) % The speed in m/s
%%
